---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import SystemsJourney from '../components/SystemsJourney.astro';
import headshot from '../assets/ahmed-avais.png';

const talksAndSessions = [
  { title: 'Human Systems Dynamics', venue: 'Agile Coach Camp Worldwide', year: '2026' },
  { title: 'Agile Transformation Simulation', venue: 'AgileIndy Conference', year: '2023' },
  { title: 'Failed Transformation Engagement: Lessons Learned', venue: 'AgileIndy Conference', year: '2023' },
  { title: 'Adaptive Action for Sustainability', venue: 'ODN Summit, Raleigh', year: '2023' },
  { title: 'Sociocracy Training Workshop', venue: 'Common Ground EcoVillage', year: '2023' },
  { title: 'Using Human Systems Dynamics', venue: 'Ignite-Agility Meetup', year: '2021' },
  { title: 'Introduction to the Agile Fluency Suite', venue: 'Agile 757', year: '2021' },
  { title: 'Coaching clinic', venue: 'Big Apple Scrum Day', year: '2021' },
  { title: "A Leader's Guide for Social Responsibility", venue: 'LASTTalk Australia', year: '2020' },
  { title: 'Up for Adoption: A Mini-Workshop Based on the Agile Fluency Game', venue: 'AgileIndy', year: '2020' },
  { title: 'High-Performance Teams (panel)', venue: 'Journyz', year: '2020' },
  { title: 'How Leaders Can Address Systemic Changes with Their Teams (panel)', venue: 'Journyz', year: '2020' },
  { title: 'Powerful Team-Based Coaching', venue: 'Agile Dialogue, Mississauga', year: '2020' },
  { title: 'Virtual Liberating Structures', venue: 'RTP Frontier', year: '2020', url: 'https://www.slideshare.net/slideshow/virtual-liberating-structures-rtp-frontier-may-2020/233687176' },
  { title: 'The Power of Inquiry', venue: 'Agile Open Northwest, Seattle', year: '2020', url: '/writing/the-power-of-inquiry' },
  { title: 'Sessions for agile coaches', venue: 'Dalian, China', year: '2019' },
  { title: 'Using the Harada Method to Develop People', venue: 'TriAgile, Raleigh', year: '2019', url: 'https://www.slideshare.net/slideshow/using-the-harada-method-to-develop-people/139352189' },
  { title: 'Custom-Tailored Agility', venue: 'Agile Leadership Network RTP', year: '2019', url: 'https://www.slideshare.net/slideshow/customtailored-agility-with-the-agile-fluency-model-137399132/137399132' },
  { title: 'Deliberate Practice: Myth, Magic, and Mindfulness', venue: 'Agile Cincy', year: '2018', url: 'https://www.slideshare.net/slideshow/deliberate-practice-myth-magic-and-mindfulness/93618888' },
  { title: 'Design Thinking in Practice', venue: 'Fitch Ratings', year: '2018', url: 'https://www.slideshare.net/slideshow/design-thinking-in-practice-88240786/88240786' },
  { title: 'The Numbers Game', venue: 'Agile Indy', year: '2017', url: 'https://www.slideshare.net/slideshow/the-numbers-game-78856239/78856239' },
];
---

<Base title="About" description="Ahmed Avais has spent more than 25 years learning how systems behave: first machines, then teams, and now teams working with AI.">
  <article class="prose">
    <header class="about-intro">
      <Image src={headshot} alt="Ahmed Avais" width={180} densities={[1, 2]} quality="high" class="headshot" loading="eager" />
      <div>
        <h1>About</h1>
        <p>I'm Ahmed Avais, and for more than 25 years I've been learning how systems behave and how to help them change. The systems started out as machines, became teams, and today they're teams working alongside AI.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <SystemsJourney />

    <h2>Machines</h2>
    <p>I began as an engineer, first running networks and then spending more than a decade on mission-critical infrastructure, where downtime affected thousands of people every day. That's where I learned to look for the constraint in a system and improve the flow around it, a habit I still bring to every problem.</p>

    <h2>Teams</h2>
    <p>In 2008 I started Agile 7 to build products with small businesses and startups. Chicago's startup scene was taking off, in the years that produced Groupon, Belly and SpotHero, and I learned a great deal from its Lean Startup and agile meetups. Extreme Programming and test-driven development showed me that code gets better when a team's habits get better, and that idea pulled me into coaching.</p>
    <p>For the next decade I coached teams and leaders in finance, healthcare and technology, sometimes with a single team and sometimes across more than 200. I worked alongside CIOs and their leadership teams, led groups of coaches, and helped managers become coaches themselves. I've led transformations with Scrum and Kanban, and along the way I've gone deep into Human Systems Dynamics. No single method fits every context, so I pick the one that fits the people and the problem.</p>
    <p>Whatever the method, I start with people as they are and help them see their own system, so they can chart their own path.</p>

    <h2>Teams and AI</h2>
    <p>More recently I helped a federal agency take a secure LLM solution from concept to production, and today I lead teams that build with AI every day. We work with specs, an agent harness and a safety net that catches what generated code gets wrong, and we've watched the constraint move from writing code to reviewing it. The human side of that shift is the hardest part, and it's where my years of coaching matter most.</p>
    <p>My teams do the work, and my part is to create the conditions for it: the practices, feedback loops and trust that let them do it well.</p>

    <h2>How I write</h2>
    <p>I write with AI the way I lead teams that build with it. The ideas, stories and judgment come from 25 years of my own work. I draft with an AI writing partner, then shape, cut and correct until it says what I mean. I don't hide it, any more than I'd hide using a calculator. AI is here to assist people, not replace them, and how we work well together is what I write about.</p>

    <h2>Talks and sessions</h2>
    <ul class="talks">
      {talksAndSessions.map((talk) => (
        <li><cite>{talk.url ? <a href={talk.url}>{talk.title}</a> : talk.title}</cite> <span class="talk-where">{talk.venue}, {talk.year}</span></li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>Credentials</h2>
    <p>I'm a Certified Enterprise Coach (CEC) and an ICF Professional Certified Coach (PCC), and I contributed to and edited <cite>The Leader's Guide for Social Responsibility</cite>. I've also published two <a href="https://miro.com/templates/profile/ahmed-avais/">Miro templates</a>, a balance wheel and an agile adoption roadmap, that people have used more than 1,500 times.</p>

    <p>I live in Apex, North Carolina, with my wife and children. The full timeline is on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/ahmedavais">LinkedIn</a>, and I make small interactive things at <a href="https://ahmedavais.com">ahmedavais.com</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
